here is the runing command to
docker rm -f nginx wordpress mariadb 2>/dev/null || true
docker network rm inception_net 2>/dev/null || true

docker volume create db_data
docker volume create wp_data
docker network create inception_net

docker build -t inception-mariadb src/requirements/mariadb
docker build -t inception-wordpress src/requirements/wordpress
docker build -t inception-nginx src/requirements/nginx

docker run -d --name mariadb \
  --network inception_net \
  -v db_data:/var/lib/mysql \
  -v "$PWD/secrets:/run/secrets:ro" \
  -e MARIADB_ROOT_PASSWORD=rayanelam \
  -e MARIADB_DATABASE=wordpress \
  -e MARIADB_USER=user \
  -e MARIADB_PASSWORD=rayanelam \
  inception-mariadb

docker run -d --name wordpress \
  --network inception_net \
  -v wp_data:/var/www/html \
  -v "$PWD/secrets:/run/secrets:ro" \
  -e MYSQL_USER=user \
  -e MYSQL_PASSWORD=rayanelam \
  -e MYSQL_DATABASE=wordpress \
  -e WP_TITLE="My Site" \
  -e DOMAIN_NAME="https://127.0.0.1:8443" \
  -e WP_ADMIN_USER=admin \
  -e WP_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com \
  -e WP_USER=author \
  -e WP_USER_EMAIL=author@example.com \
  -e WP_USER_PASSWORD=rayanelam \
  inception-wordpress

docker run -d --name nginx \
  --network inception_net \
  -p 8443:443 \
  -v wp_data:/var/www/html \
  inception-nginx

this is the work that we need to do every time without docker-compose
creating the volumes, network connect containers ...
dockercompose.yaml make it easy on us cu he manage all of that



mistakes that broke the servers:
1. using a bad base image name in the nginx Dockerfile (`debain` instead of `debian`)
2. exposing the wrong nginx port (`80` while the server listens on `443`)
3. putting the nginx server block in the wrong file at first (`nginx.conf` instead of a vhost file in `conf.d/default.conf`)
4. starting nginx with a WordPress upstream that was not reachable from a plain `docker run` setup
5. using the wrong hostname in WordPress and nginx (`localhost` vs `127.0.0.1`)
6. storing WordPress site URLs that did not match the browser URL, which caused redirect loops
7. not quoting `WP_TITLE` in the WordPress install command, so `My Site` broke the install
8. reusing old containers, networks, and volumes without cleaning them first
9. forgetting to mount and read the secret files from `/run/secrets`















